10. Practical incident stories to memorize
Story A — P99 spike but not GPU issue

Symptom: Fraud hot path p99 jumped from 8ms to 47ms on one node.

Investigation:

htop showed housekeeping core saturated
ps showed monitoring agents and irqbalance
perf showed high TLB misses
numastat showed remote NUMA memory
VTune confirmed remote DRAM access
STREAM showed hardware memory bandwidth was healthy

Root cause:

IRQ affinity broken
HugePages missing after reboot
memory allocated cross-NUMA

Fix:

disable irqbalance
pin NIC IRQs to housekeeping cores
restore HugePages
bind process CPU/memory to correct NUMA node
add boot/runtime validation

Lesson:

“A GPU/AI p99 issue can be caused by CPU scheduling, interrupts, TLB misses, or NUMA. I validate the host before blaming the model.”

Story B — LLM decode slow

Symptom: vLLM throughput lower than expected, TPOT high, nvidia-smi looked okay.

Investigation:

runtime metrics showed TPOT high
Nsight Systems showed kernel launch gaps
logs showed enforce_eager/CUDA Graphs disabled
Nsight Compute showed memory-bound paged attention
KV cache was FP16 instead of FP8
NCCL/DCGM showed hardware healthy

Fix:

enable CUDA Graphs
use FP8 KV cache
enable prefix caching
enable chunked prefill
tune max_num_seqs and max_num_batched_tokens

Lesson:

“nvidia-smi utilization is not enough. I use runtime metrics for symptoms, Nsight Systems for timeline gaps, and Nsight Compute for kernel bottlenecks.”

Story C — Model loading slow

Symptom: Pod restart took 5 minutes before model ready.

Investigation:

startup logs timed model weight load
FIO showed NVMe healthy
logs showed model downloading remotely
iostat showed NVMe underutilized after local cache

Fix:

pre-cache model on local NVMe
verify model in init container
readiness only after model load + warmup
use warm pools
consider GPUDirect Storage for large models

Lesson:

“For AI serving, model readiness means model loaded, KV cache initialized, CUDA Graphs captured, and first inference warmed — not just container started.”

Story D — Shared GPU OOM with BERT models

Symptom: Multiple BERT/ONNX models on one GPU intermittently OOM.

Investigation:

tag request by model/version/tenant/input length/batch size
run each model alone and in combinations
DCGM/Nsight show memory spike timing
PyTorch profiler shows activation memory and padding/sequence-length issue

Fix:

cap max sequence length
reduce padding with dynamic padding
bucket by sequence length
lower max batch size for heavy model
FP16/BF16 or TensorRT
separate batching policy
dedicated GPU/MIG for high-memory model
add peak-memory canary gate

Lesson:

“Shared-GPU OOM is controlled by peak memory per model, not average GPU memory or model weight size.”

11. Your final interview framework

When asked any troubleshooting question, answer in this pattern:

1. Clarify symptom:
   Is it latency, throughput, OOM, error rate, cost, or quality?

2. Localize:
   Which model, tenant, node, GPU, input shape, deployment version?

3. Split latency:
   queue, preprocessing, runtime, GPU, copy, network, downstream

4. Apply USE/RED:
   Service health and infrastructure health

5. Choose profiler:
   PyTorch profiler for model ops
   Nsight Systems for timeline
   Nsight Compute for kernels
   perf/VTune for CPU
   FIO/STREAM/NCCL for baselines

6. Fix safely:
   immediate mitigation, root-cause fix, prevention gate

7. Validate:
   p99, throughput, cost, error rate, quality regression
